# tools/cr/crypto_ed25519_scalarmul_witness.rish -- scalar multiplication on edwards25519 authored in pure Rye, composed entirely over the GREEN
# group law (crypto/ed25519_group.rye) and, beneath it, the GREEN base field (crypto/fe25519.rye): Season G's eighth rung, k-P by the
# double-and-add ladder, proven against algebraic known-answers and byte-for-byte against an independent implementation (Season G -
# Cryptography).
#
# Season: the Six-Season double-seat, Season G thread (Cryptography - Rye-native, Monocypher-parity, audit-ready).
# Design read: active-designing/date/20260815/20260815-175524_rye-first-crypto-parity-and-the-decision-wave.md (Season G names Ed25519 as the Lotus unblock,
# its scalar multiplication a composition over the group law).
# Kin: crypto/ed25519_group.rye (the group law, GREEN) - crypto/fe25519.rye (the base field, GREEN) - crypto/poly1305.rye - crypto/aead.rye.
# Monocypher (vendor/monocypher, CC0/BSD-dual) is the parity TARGET, never a copied line -- our own composition, studied clean-room from the
# public mathematics of twisted Edwards curves and Monocypher's public API (.claude/rules/gratitude-licenses.md).
#
# crypto/ed25519_scalarmul.rye computes k-P by walking the 32-byte little-endian scalar from its most significant bit down, doubling the
# accumulator at every bit and adding P where the bit is set, starting from the group identity (X = 0, Y = 1, Z = 1, T = 0). It composes only
# point doubling and point addition -- no field inversion, no new curve math. The ladder is deliberately variable-time (it branches on scalar
# bits), correct but not yet timing-safe; a constant-time ladder for secret scalars stays a separate named horizon. The primitive calls no
# std.crypto. Parity is proven at selftest against two references runnable now: algebraic known-answers (0-B is the identity, 1-B is B, 2-B is
# the doubling of B, (k+1)-B is k-B plus B), and Zig's own std.crypto.ecc.Edwards25519.mul as an independent implementation, byte-for-byte on
# the compression of k-P across a spread of scalars over both the base point and 3-B. The vendored Monocypher source is a held network fetch,
# so Monocypher-source parity is an honest horizon rung. Purely local -- a scalar and a point are arithmetic, not a secret and not a signature:
# no real key, no signing (Ed25519's signing stays its own gated round), no network, no funds, no real device; constant-time timing-safety a
# separate named horizon.
#
# Run from the repository root:
#   rishi/bin/rishi run tools/cr/crypto_ed25519_scalarmul_witness.rish

say "crypto-ed25519-scalarmul: Language EN — Season G's eighth rung: scalar multiplication on edwards25519 authored in pure Rye, composed entirely over the GREEN group law (crypto/ed25519_group.rye) and, beneath it, the GREEN base field (crypto/fe25519.rye). Ed25519 signing — the Lotus signed carry, Kumara identity, Vault, Comlink — derives the public key A = a·B and the commitment R = r·B by adding the base point to itself a scalar number of times; this rung authors exactly that operation, k·P, as a composition over the group's two primitives, point doubling and point addition. It is pure arithmetic — no key, no signature, no identity, no custody gate — so once it is parity-GREEN, Ed25519's public-key derivation and commitment become compositions over this ladder."
say "crypto-ed25519-scalarmul: Style Radiant — the law is stated positively: k·P is P added to itself k times, computed by the double-and-add ladder in a number of group operations proportional to the scalar's bit length; walk the 32-byte little-endian scalar from its high bit down, double the accumulator at every bit and add P where the bit is set, starting from the group identity (X = 0, Y = 1, Z = 1, T = 0). Because the base point has prime order L, a 32-byte scalar above L simply lands on (k mod L)·P — the same point a reduced scalar would."
say "crypto-ed25519-scalarmul: Lens TAME — the primitive calls no std.crypto; it composes over the group law's proven public API, adding no field inversion and no new curve math, and asserts its contracts on every function. The ladder is deliberately variable-time (it branches on scalar bits), correct but not yet timing-safe; a constant-time ladder for secret scalars stays a separate named horizon. Parity holds against algebraic known-answers AND Zig's independent std.crypto.ecc.Edwards25519.mul byte-for-byte on the compression of k·P across a spread of scalars over both the base point and 3·B. Monocypher-source parity is a named horizon behind the held vendor fetch. Purely local — a scalar and a point are not a secret and not a signature, so no real key, no signing, no network, no funds, no device."

let zig = "vendor/zig-toolchain/zig"
let bin = "crypto/bin/ed25519_scalarmul"

let build = run ["sh" "-c" "mkdir -p crypto/bin && env RYE_ZIG=${zig} rye/bin/rye build crypto/ed25519_scalarmul.rye -femit-bin=${bin}"]
assert build.ok else "crypto-ed25519-scalarmul: build failed — ${build.err}"

let outfile = "crypto/bin/ed25519_scalarmul.out"
let selftest = run ["sh" "-c" "${bin} selftest > ${outfile} 2>&1"]
assert selftest.ok else "crypto-ed25519-scalarmul: selftest exited non-zero — see ${outfile}"

let shown = run ["sh" "-c" "cat ${outfile}"]
say "crypto-ed25519-scalarmul: ${shown.out}"

let green = run ["sh" "-c" "grep -q 'GREEN crypto-ed25519-scalarmul' ${outfile} && echo yes"]
assert (trim green.out) == "yes" else "crypto-ed25519-scalarmul: selftest did not print the GREEN crypto-ed25519-scalarmul line"
